architecture snapshot
Frontend (src): A Vite-powered React 18 SPA written in TypeScript, styled with TailwindCSS and animated via Framer Motion. The app is split into landing views and the core application shell.

PDFContext tracks the uploaded file, pagination, zoom, upload state, and the backend-issued sessionId.
ChatContext orchestrates the conversational flow, pushing messages into state, streaming responses, and decorating assistant replies with page references.
PDFUploader validates PDFs (<10 MB), calls uploadPDF, then seeds PDFContext with the new session metadata; PDFViewer renders pages through react-pdf while keeping pagination/zoom in sync with context.
ChatInterface renders the threaded conversation, formats assistant answers with react-markdown + remark-gfm, offers copy-to-clipboard, and exposes canned FAQs that call sendMessage.
HTTP integration lives in ragService.ts, which reads VITE_API_BASE_URL and wraps /upload, /chat, /sessions, /health, and /session/{id}; the service expects ChatContext to provide the active session.
Backend (Backend): A FastAPI service (BackendServer.py) exposing /upload and /chat endpoints plus lightweight session management.

PDFProcessor combines pdfplumber/PyPDF2 for extraction with LangChain’s RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter to produce overlapping 1 000-char chunks.
VectorStore lazily loads SentenceTransformers (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) and FAISS, persists the index in faiss_store, and returns similarity-ranked snippets for each query.
LLMService instantiates either OpenAI or Anthropic clients, wraps context into a RAG prompt, enforces concise Markdown answers, and attaches page-level references drawn from the retrieved chunks.
Configuration is centralized in config.py via pydantic-settings, controlling ports, chunk sizing, CORS origins, embedding model, and provider API keys from .env.
Sessions are kept in-memory for now (with a note to swap for Redis/DB in production); deleting the last session clears the FAISS index.
Data flow:

Upload: SPA → /upload → PDF extraction/chunking → embeddings → FAISS persist → session metadata returned to client.
Question: SPA → /chat → FAISS search → top chunks contextualize LLM → formatted Markdown response plus reference list → UI renders via ReactMarkdown.
Dependencies & tooling: Frontend dependencies (see package.json) include react-markdown, remark-gfm, react-pdf, uuid, and lucide-react. Backend dependencies (requirements.txt) cover FastAPI, uvicorn, SentenceTransformers, FAISS, LangChain, OpenAI, and Anthropic SDKs.







Balancing rich Markdown rendering with Tailwind styling meant building custom ReactMarkdown component overrides and aggressively testing edge-case formatting (lists, code blocks, nested headings) so the chat UI stayed readable.
The FAISS + SentenceTransformers stack proved sensitive to dependency versions and lazy initialization order; we had to rework initialization to avoid import-time crashes and ensure the embeddings index persisted safely.
Prompt-tuning for concise, page-referenced answers required several iterations—lowering temperature, tightening instructions, and synthesizing context metadata—to keep responses both professional and cite-aware.
Handling large PDF uploads surfaced performance and validation issues; chunking thresholds, size guards, and user-facing feedback (progress, errors) all had to be tuned to keep the upload/processing flow resilient.